 for full-attention layers\n4. Add YaRN RoPE for full-attention layers\n\nLet me also check what the mlx_lm base model looks like and how models register themselves.\n\nActually, looking at the existing MLX spark2_5.py more carefully, it's already an MLX port but with some differences from the transformers version:\n- MLX version: q_k_v_proj, g_proj, out_proj, rope per layer\n- Transformer version: same but with position_embeddings passed differently\n\nThe key differences I need to handle:\n1. The transformers version computes RoPE cos/sin once per layer type and passes them as position_embeddings\n2. The MLX version uses nn.RoPE per layer\n3. The transformers version uses eager_attention_forward with repeat_kv\n4. The MLX version uses scaled_dot_product_attention\n\nFor my custom modeling_spark.py, I should:\n- Follow mlx-lm conventions (so `mlx_lm.load` can find and use it)\n- Use the ModelArgs pattern from the existing MLX spark2_5.py\n- Add the three patches\n\nLet me also check how `mlx_lm.load` discovers models - it uses `auto_map` from config.json to find the model class.\n\nLooking at config.json:\n```json\n\"auto_map\": {\n    \"AutoConfig\": \"configuration_spark.Spark2_5Config\",\n    \"AutoModel\": \"modeling_spark.Spark2_5Model\",\n    \"AutoModelForCausalLM\": \"modeling_spark.Spark2_5ForCausalLM\"\n}\n```\n\nAnd the MLX spark2_5.py uses class names `ModelArgs`, `Attention`, `MLP`, `TransformerBlock`, `Spark2_5Model`, `Model`.\n\nFor `mlx_lm.load`, it needs:\n- A `ModelArgs` dataclass\n- A `Model` class (or the architecture class from auto_map)\n\nLet me write the custom modeling_spark.py now. I'll base it on the existing MLX spark2_5.py but port the transformers-style architecture with the three patches.\n\nLet me plan the structure:\n\n```python\n# modeling_spark.py — custom MLX port of transformers-style Spark2_5\n# with three runtime patches:\n#   (a) Hadamard weight rotation applied to activations\n#   (b) TurboQuant KV-cache rotation for full-attention layers\n#   (c) YaRN RoPE scaling for full-attention layers\n\nimport json, math\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional\n\nimport mlx.core as mx\nimport mlx.nn as nn\n\nfrom mlx_lm.models.base import BaseModelArgs, create_attention_mask, scaled_dot_product_attention\nfrom mlx_lm.models.cache import KVCache, RotatingKVCache\nfrom mlx_lm.models.rope_utils import YarnRoPE, initialize_rope\n\n\nBLOCK = 64  # Hadamard rotation block size\n\ndef hadamard_block(d):\n    \"\"\"Build a d×d Hadamard matrix (d must be a power of two).\"\"\"\n    H = mx.array([[1.0]])\n    while H.shape[0] < d:\n        H = mx.block([[H, H], [H, -H]])\n    return H / mx.sqrt(mx.array(d, dtype=mx.float32))\n\n# Build once at import time\nH = hadamard_block(BLOCK)  # [64, 64]\n\ndef rotate_blockwise(x, H):\n    \"\"\"Apply blockwise Hadamard rotation to the last dimension of x.\n    x: [..., in_features] where in_features % BLOCK == 0\n    Returns: [..., in_features] rotated\n    \"\"\"\n    *leading, in_f = x.shape\n    x = x.reshape(-1, in_f // BLOCK, BLOCK)  # [*, n_blocks, BLOCK]\n    # x @ H^T per block: [..., n_blocks, BLOCK] @ [BLOCK, BLOCK] -> [..., n_blocks, BLOCK]\n    x_rot = mx.matmul(x, H.T)  # mx.matmul broadcasts over leading dims\n    return x_rot.reshape(*leading, in_f)\n```\n\nWait, I need to be careful with mx.matmul broadcasting. Let me think about this.\n\nIf x has shape [..., n_blocks, BLOCK] and H has shape [BLOCK, BLOCK], then mx.matmul(x, H.T) should work because mx.matmul broadcasts over leading dimensions. So x_rot will have shape [..., n_blocks, BLOCK].\n\nActually, I need to verify this. In MLX, `mx.matmul` does support broadcasting over batch dimensions. So if x is [B, n_blocks, BLOCK] and H.T is [BLOCK, BLOCK], the result is [B, n_blocks, BLOCK].\n\nBut if x is [..., n_blocks, BLOCK] with more than 2 leading dims, it should also work. Let me proceed with this assumption.\n\nNow for the model architecture:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass ModelArgs(BaseModelArgs):\n    model_type: str\n    hidden_size: int\n    intermediate_size: int\n    num_hidden_layers: int\n    num_attention_heads: int\n    num_key_value_heads: int\n    head_dim: int\n    vocab_size: int\n    sliding_window: int\n    rope_parameters: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]]\n    layer_types: Optional[List[str]] = None\n    rms_norm_eps: float = 1e-6\n    max_position_embeddings: int = 1048576\n    attention_bias: bool = False\n    mlp_bias: bool = False\n    hidden_act: str = \"gelu\"\n    gate_attn_act_mode: str = \"sigmoid\"\n    headwise_attn_output_gate: bool = True\n    tie_word_embeddings: bool = True\n    \n    # Patch flags\n    use_weight_rotation: bool = True\n    use_kv_cache_rotation: bool = True\n    use_yarn_rope: bool = True\n    \n    def __post_init__(self):\n        # ... same as existing MLX spark2_5.py ...\n```\n\nNow for the Attention class with patches:\n\n```python\nclass Attention(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, args: ModelArgs, layer_idx: int):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.n_heads = args.num_attention_heads\n        self.n_kv_heads = args.num_key_value_heads\n        self.head_dim = args.head_dim\n        self.scale = self.head_dim**-0.5\n        \n        layer_type = args.layer_types[layer_idx]\n        self.is_sliding = layer_type == \"sliding_attention\"\n        self.is_full = not self.is_sliding\n        self.layer_idx = layer_idx\n        \n        q_size = self.n_heads * self.head_dim\n        kv_size = self.n_kv_heads * self.head_dim\n        self.q_size = q_size\n        self.kv_size = kv_size\n        \n        self.q_k_v_proj = nn.Linear(args.hidden_size, q_size + 2 * kv_size, bias=args.attention_bias)\n        self.g_proj = nn.Linear(args.hidden_size, self.n_heads, bias=args.attention_bias)\n        self.out_proj = nn.Linear(q_size, args.hidden_size, bias=args.attention_bias)\n        \n        # Patch (c): YaRN RoPE for full-attention layers\n        if self.is_full and args.use_yarn_rope:\n            rope_params = args.rope_parameters[layer_type]\n            base = float(rope_params.get(\"rope_theta\", 10000.0))\n            self.rope = YarnRoPE(\n                dims=int(self.head_dim * 0.25),  # partial_rotary_factor = 0.25\n                traditional=False,\n                max_position_embeddings=args.max_position_embeddings,\n                base=base,\n                scaling_factor=4.0,\n                original_max_position_embeddings=262144,\n            )\n        else:\n            rope_params = args.rope_parameters[layer_type]\n            base = float(rope_params.get(\"rope_theta\", 10000.0))\n            factor = float(rope_params.get(\"partial_rotary_factor\", 1.0))\n            dims = int(self.head_dim * factor)\n            self.rope = nn.RoPE(dims, traditional=False, base=base)\n        \n        # Patch (a): Hadamard rotation matrix (stored as buffer)\n        self.H = H  # reference to the global Hadamard matrix\n    \n    def __call__(self, x, mask=None, cache=None):\n        batch_size, sequence_length, _ = x.shape\n        \n        # Patch (a): Apply Hadamard rotation to hidden_states before q_k_v_proj\n        if self.is_full and args.use_weight_rotation:\n            x_rot = rotate_blockwise(x, self.H)\n        else:\n            x_rot = x\n        \n        qkv = self.q_k_v_proj(x_rot)\n        queries, keys, values = mx.split(qkv, [self.q_size, self.q_size + self.kv_size], axis=-1)\n        \n        queries = queries.reshape(batch_size, sequence_length, self.n_heads, self.head_dim).transpose(0, 2, 1, 3)\n        keys = keys.reshape(batch_size, sequence_length, self.n_kv_heads, self.head_dim).transpose(0, 2, 1, 3)\n        values = values.reshape(batch_size, sequence_length, self.n_kv_heads, self.head_dim).transpose(0, 2, 1, 3)\n        \n        queries = mx.contiguous(queries)\n        keys = mx.contiguous(keys)\n        values = mx.contiguous(values)\n        \n        # RoPE\n        offset = cache.offset if cache is not None else 0\n        queries = self.rope(queries, offset=offset)\n        keys = self.rope(keys, offset=offset)\n        \n        # Patch (b): KV cache rotation\n        if self.is_full and args.use_kv_cache_rotation and cache is not None:\n            # Rotate query to match rotated key frame\n            # keys stored in cache are already in rotated frame\n            # We need to rotate queries to match\n            q_rot = rotate_blockwise_query(queries, self.H)  # rotate query in head_dim space\n            # Actually, we need to rotate query per head per head_dim\n            ...\n        \n        if cache is not None:\n            keys, values = cache.update_and_fetch(keys, values)\n        \n        output = scaled_dot_product_attention(queries, keys, values, cache=cache, scale=self.scale, mask=mask)\n        output = mx.contiguous(output.transpose(0, 2, 1, 3))\n        \n        gate = mx.sigmoid(self.g_proj(x).astype(mx.float32)).astype(output.dtype)\n        output = output * gate[..., None]\n        \n        return self.out_proj(output.reshape(batch_size, sequence_length, -1))\n```\n\nWait, I need to think more carefully about the KV cache rotation.\n\nThe idea from TurboQuant: If we rotate the keys with H (applied to the head_dim dimension), and rotate the queries with H as well, then:\n- q^T k = (Hq)^T (Hk) = q^T H^T H k = q^T k (since H is orthogonal)\n\nSo the attention scores are unchanged. We store keys in the rotated frame (Hk) and rotate queries (Hq) at inference time.\n\nBut wait - the Hadamard rotation we applied to the weights was on the INPUT dimension of q_k_v_proj. The weights were rotated as W @ H^T where H is applied to the hidden_size dimension (2560). This means at runtime, we rotate the hidden_states (input to q_k_v_proj) with H before the projection.\n\nFor the KV cache rotation, we're rotating the KEYS in the head_dim dimension (256), not the hidden_size dimension. This is a different rotation. Let me re-read the roadmap...\n\nFrom the roadmap:\n> \"The key insight is: rotate the query once, un-rotate the output once, and attention scores are unchanged because the rotation is orthogonal.\"\n\n> \"TurboQuantKVCache stores keys in the rotated frame and handles the query rotation internally.\"\n\nSo the KV cache rotation applies H to the head_dim dimension of keys and queries. The head_dim is 256, which is a power of two, so we can use a full 256×256 Hadamard matrix, or blockwise with 64.\n\nWait, but we already used a 64×64 blockwise Hadamard for the weight rotation on the hidden_size dimension (2560 = 40 × 64). For the KV cache rotation, we'd apply H to the head_dim dimension (256). Since 256 is a power of two, we can use a full 256×256 Hadamard, or blockwise 64×64 blocks (4 blocks of 64).\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully: the weight rotation H is applied to the hidden_size dimension (2560) using 64×64 blocks. The KV cache rotation would apply a (potentially different) H to the head_dim dimension (256).\n\nFor simplicity and consistency, let me use the same block size (64) for both. For head_dim=256, that's 4 blocks of 64.\n\nLet me think about the KV cache rotation more carefully:\n\nIn the standard flow:\n1. q, k, v are computed from the projection\n2. RoPE is applied to q and k\n3. keys and values are stored in cache\n4. Attention: q @ k^T\n\nWith KV cache rotation:\n1. q, k, v are computed from the projection  \n2. RoPE is applied to q and k\n3. Keys are rotated: k_rot = k @ H_head^T (where H_head is Hadamard on head_dim)\n4. k_rot is stored in cache\n5. Query is rotated: q_rot = q @ H_head^T\n6. Attention: q_rot @ k_rot^T = q @ H_head^T @ H_head @ k^T = q @ k^T (since H is orthogonal)\n\nSo the attention scores are identical. The benefit is that k_rot has a more Gaussian distribution, making quantization more accurate.\n\nFor the implementation:\n- When storing keys in cache: rotate them first\n- When fetching queries: rotate them to match\n\nBut wait - the cache stores both keys and values. We only rotate keys (not values), because values don't participate in the attention score computation directly (they're weighted by the attention probabilities).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The standard KVCache.update_and_fetch takes keys and values and returns the full keys and values from cache. If we rotate keys before storing, we need to:\n1. Rotate keys before passing to cache.update_and_fetch\n2. Rotate queries to match before attention\n\nBut the cache returns keys for the attention computation. So:\n- cache stores rotated keys\n- cache returns rotated keys\n- We rotate queries to match\n- Attention: q_rot @ k_rot^T = q @ k^T ✓\n\nValues are stored and returned as-is (no rotation needed).\n\nNow, there's a subtlety: the `scaled_dot_product_attention` function in MLX takes the cache and handles the masking internally. Let me check how it uses the keys from the cache.\n\nLooking at the existing MLX spark2_5.py:\n```python\nif cache is not None:\n    keys, values = cache.update_and_fetch(keys, values)\n\noutput = scaled_dot_product_attention(\n    queries, keys, values, cache=cache, scale=self.scale, mask=mask,\n)\n```\n\nSo the cache.update_and_fetch returns the full keys and values (including cached ones), and then scaled_dot_product_attention uses them directly. The cache parameter is also passed for masking purposes.\n\nSo my approach:\n1. Compute q, k, v from projection\n2. Apply RoPE to q and k\n3. If KV cache rotation is enabled and this is a full-attention layer:\n   - Rotate k: k = k @ H_head^T\n   - Rotate q: q = q @ H_head^T\n4. Pass rotated k to cache.update_and_fetch\n5. Cache returns rotated k (and v)\n6. Attention uses rotated q and rotated k → same scores as original\n\nWait, but there's a problem with step 3. If I rotate k before passing to cache, then cache stores rotated k. But when cache returns keys for attention, those keys are already rotated. And I've also rotated q. So q_rot @ k_rot^T = q @ k^T. ✓\n\nBut what about when the cache already has previous keys? Those previous keys were also rotated when they were stored. So when cache.update_and_fetch returns all keys (cached + new), they're all in the rotated frame. And q_rot matches. ✓\n\nOK, let me also think about the RoPE interaction. RoPE is applied BEFORE the KV cache rotation in my flow above. Is that correct?\n\nThe roadmap says: \"rotate the query once, un-rotate the output once\". It doesn't specify the order relative to RoPE. But mathematically, since both RoPE and Hadamard are linear orthogonal transformations, they commute in the sense that:\n- q_rot_rope = RoPE(H @ q) = H @ RoPE(q) (if H and RoPE commute)\n\nBut they don't necessarily commute! RoPE applies different rotations to different position pairs, while H is a fixed orthogonal matrix. So RoPE(Hq) ≠ H(RoPE(q)) in general.\n\nHowever, the key insight is that we want q_rot^T k_rot = q^T k. If we apply both RoPE and H to both q and k:\n- q' = H @ RoPE(q)\n- k' = H @ RoPE(k)\n- q'^T k' = RoPE(q)^T H^T H RoPE(k) = RoPE(q)^T RoPE(k)\n\nAnd RoPE is also orthogonal (it's a rotation), so RoPE(q)^T RoPE(k) = q^T k. ✓\n\nSo the order doesn't matter as long as we apply the same transformations to both q and k. Whether we do RoPE then H, or H then RoPE, the result is the same because both are orthogonal.\n\nWait, actually that's not quite right. RoPE is orthogonal for each position independently, but the rotation angle depends on position. So RoPE(q) at position i uses a different rotation than RoPE(q) at position j. But H is the same for all positions. So:\n\nq'_i = H @ RoPE_i(q_i)\nk'_j = H @ RoPE_j(k_j)\nq'_i^T k'_j = RoPE_i(q_i)^T H^T H RoPE_j(k_j) = RoPE_i(q_i)^T RoPE_j(k_j)\n\nBut RoPE_i(q_i)^T RoPE_j(k_j) ≠ q_i^T k_j in general, because RoPE_i and RoPE_j are different rotations!\n\nHmm, but this is the same as the original computation: q_i^T k_j where q_i = RoPE_i(q_i_orig) and k_j = RoPE_j(k_j_orig). So:\n\nq'_i^T k'_j = RoPE_i(q_i_orig)^T RoPE_j(k_j_orig) = q_i^T k_j\n\nSo yes, the attention scores are identical regardless of whether we apply H before or after RoPE, as long as we apply the same H to both q and k. ✓\n\nGreat, so my approach is correct. Let me now write the complete modeling_spark.py.\n\nActually, wait. I need to reconsider where to apply the KV cache rotation relative to RoPE and cache storage.\n\nThe cleanest approach:\n1. Compute q, k, v from projection\n2. Apply RoPE to q and k (this is needed for position encoding)\n3. If KV cache rotation: rotate both q and k with H_head\n4. Store rotated k in cache\n5. Cache returns rotated k\n6. Attention with rotated q and rotated k\n\nThis way, the cache stores rotated keys, and queries are rotated to match. The attention scores are identical to the unrotated version.\n\nNow, for the `rotate_blockwise_query` function - I need to apply H to the head_dim dimension of q and k. The shape of q is [batch, n_heads, seq_len, head_dim]. I need to rotate the last dimension (head_dim) in blocks of 64.\n\nSince head_dim = 256 = 4 × 64, I can reshape to [..., 4, 64] and apply the 64×64 Hadamard to each block.\n\nLet me write the rotation function for the head_dim:\n\n```python\ndef rotate_head_dim(x, H):\n    \"\"\"Apply blockwise Hadamard rotation to the last dimension (head_dim).\n    x: [..., head_dim] where head_dim % BLOCK == 0\n    \"\"\"\n    *leading, head_dim = x.shape\n    x = x.reshape(*leading, head_dim // BLOCK, BLOCK)\n    x_rot = mx.matmul(x, H.T)  # [..., n_blocks, BLOCK] @ [BLOCK, BLOCK]\n    return x_rot.reshape(*leading, head_dim)\n```\n\nThis should work with mx.matmul broadcasting.\n\nNow, there's another subtlety with the KV cache rotation. When we fetch keys from the cache for attention, the cache returns keys that include both cached (previously stored, already rotated) and newly stored (just rotated) keys. All are in the rotated frame. And q is also rotated. So q_rot @ k_rot^T gives the correct scores.\n\nBut wait - the `scaled_dot_product_attention` function in mlx_lm might also use the cache for masking. Let me check if passing rotated keys to the cache causes any issues with the mask.\n\nLooking at KVCache.update_and_fetch: it just stores and returns keys/values. The mask is created separately by create_attention_mask. So rotating keys before storing shouldn't affect masking.\n\nOK, I think my approach is sound. Let me now write the complete modeling_spark.py.\n\nOne more thing: I need to handle the case where the cache is a TurboQuantKVCache that does quantization. Looking at KVCache.to_quantized(), it quantizes both keys and values. If we're storing rotated keys, the quantized rotated keys will have a more Gaussian distribution, which is the whole point.\n\nBut actually, for now, I don't need to implement quantization of the KV cache itself - just the rotation. The roadmap mentions TurboQuantKVCache but says it's a \"drop-in replacement\" for the standard cache class. Since we're writing a custom modeling_spark.py, I'll implement the rotation logic directly.\n\nLet me also think about whether I need a separate cache class or can just modify the Attention forward pass. I think modifying the forward pass is simpler:\n\n1. Before calling cache.update_and_fetch, rotate keys\n2. Rotate queries to match\n3. Everything else stays the same\n\nThis way, I can use the standard KVCache class but with rotated keys.\n\nBut there's a problem: if I rotate keys before storing them, the cache stores rotated keys. When the cache returns keys (for the attention computation), they're rotated. But what if something else expects unrotated keys? In the standard flow, only the attention computation uses the keys from the cache, so this should be fine.\n\nOK let me write the complete modeling_spark.py now. I'll base it on the existing MLX spark2_5.py but with the transformers-style architecture and the three patches.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the architecture. The existing MLX spark2_5.py already works with the quantized model (it was used for the MLX convert). But it uses nn.RoPE per layer and the ModelArgs pattern. The transformers-style model uses a different RoPE computation (cos/sin precomputed per layer type).\n\nFor my custom modeling_spark.py, I should:\n1. Keep the MLX spark2_5.py structure (ModelArgs, Attention, MLP, etc.) since it's already compatible with mlx_lm.load\n2. Add the three patches to it\n3. Make sure the config.json's auto_map points to the right classes\n\nWait, but the config.json says:\n```json\n\"auto_map\": {\n    \"AutoModel\": \"modeling_spark.Spark2_5Model\",\n    \"AutoModelForCausalLM\": \"modeling_spark.Spark2_5ForCausalLM\"\n}\n```\n\nBut the MLX spark2_5.py uses class name `Model`, not `Spark2_5Model` or `Spark2_5ForCausalLM`. The MLX `mlx_lm.load` function doesn't use auto_map the same way transformers does - it has its own model discovery mechanism.\n\nLet me check how mlx_lm.load discovers models...\n\nActually, looking at the mlx-lm source, `mlx_lm.load` looks for a `model.py` or uses the `model_type` from config to find the model class in `mlx_lm.models`. It doesn't use the transformers auto_map.\n\nSo for `mlx_lm.load` to work with my custom modeling_spark.py, I need to either:\n1. Place it in `mlx_lm/models/spark2_5.py` (replacing the existing one)\n2. Or use a different mechanism\n\nActually, looking more carefully at how mlx_lm.load works, it imports the model module based on `model_type` from config.json. For `model_type = \"spark2_5\"`, it would look for `mlx_lm.models.spark2_5`. But since we're using `trust_remote_code=True`, it might also look for a local `modeling_spark.py`.\n\nLet me check the mlx_lm load function to understand how it discovers custom models.\n\nActually, I think the simplest approach is to write the custom `modeling_spark.py` in the model directory and make `mlx_lm.load` find it. Let me check how mlx-lm handles `trust_remote_code` and custom model files.\n\nLooking at the