 in_f // BLOCK\n    x = x.reshape(*leading, n_blocks, BLOCK)\n    x_rot = mx.matmul(x, H.T)\n    return x_rot.reshape(*leading, in_f).astype(orig_dtype)\n```\n\nThis computes x @ H^T per block. But the weight rotation was W @ H^T per block. So:\n- Weight: W_rot[b, i, j] = sum_k W[b, i, k] * H[j, k] = (W @ H^T)[b, i, j]\n- Activation: x_rot[b, j] = sum_k x[b, k] * H[j, k] = (H @ x)[b, j]... wait\n\nActually, let me be more careful. x @ H^T means:\nx_rot[b, j] = sum_k x[b, k] * H^T[k, j] = sum_k x[b, k] * H[j, k]\n\nAnd W @ H^T means:\nW_rot[b, j] = sum_k W[b, k] * H^T[k, j] = sum_k W[b, k] * H[j, k]\n\nSo both use H[j, k] as the coefficient. Then:\nW_rot @ x_rot = sum_j W_rot[b, j] * x_rot[b, j]\n= sum_j (sum_k W[b, k] * H[j, k]) * (sum_l x[b, l] * H[j, l])\n= sum_j sum_k sum_l W[b, k] * H[j, k] * x[b, l] * H[j, l]\n\nSince H is orthogonal: sum_j H[j, k] * H[j, l] = δ[k, l]\n\nSo: = sum_k sum_l W[b, k] * x[b, l] * δ[k, l] = sum_k W[b, k] * x[b, k] = (W @ x)[b]\n\n✓ This is correct!\n\nSo the rotation math is correct. Let me check if there's an implementation bug...\n\nActually, wait. Let me check `mx.matmul` behavior. For x with shape [..., n_blocks, BLOCK] and H.T with shape [BLOCK, BLOCK]:\n\nmx.matmul(x, H.T) computes [..., n_blocks, BLOCK] @ [BLOCK, BLOCK]\n\nBut does MLX's matmul broadcast correctly over [..., n_blocks]? Let me check...\n\nIn MLX, `mx.matmul` supports broadcasting for the batch dimensions. So if x is [B, S, 40, 64] and H.T is [64, 64], the result should be [B, S, 40, 64].\n\nBut wait - [B, S, 40, 64] @ [64, 64] - is this treated as batched matmul where each [64] vector is multiplied by [64, 64]? Or is it treated as [B*S*40, 64] @ [64, 64]?\n\nIn MLX, matmul with more than 2 dimensions treats all but the last 2 as batch dimensions. So [B, S, 40, 64] @ [64, 64] → [B, S, 40, 64]. Each [1, 64] vector is multiplied by [64, 64], giving [1, 64]. This is correct.\n\nBut wait, [B, S, 40, 64] means the last 2 dimensions are [40, 64]. So matmul treats this