        "displayText": "so whats next?\n\n\nwhat do you need?\n\n\n\nso umm\n\n\nyeah\n\n\n(from grok)\nthis is for the jinja template to work\n\n**Got them.** Here’s the read on the only run that matters: `probe_20261101T025205Z` (20 real generations). The other two are dead (top_k fail + dry-run).\n\n### Template plumbing — solid\n\n| Check | Result |\n|-------|--------|\n| FRAME CARD always present | yes (every row) |\n| `helpful assistant` | never |\n| Forced coder flag when `construct=coder` | yes |\n| Forced coder flag when *not* coder | no |\n| Model loads on MPS and generates | yes (~13–15s/job) |\n\n### Model behaviour — the actual problem\n\n**With thinking on (`effort=low/medium/high/soft`):**\n\nAlmost every answer starts with frame *announcement* in the thinking channel:\n\n- “Construct: coder is a good fit…”\n- “Coder mode is forced…”\n- “AI engineer mode is forced, but… let me pick coder”\n- “Logic mode is forced though… I should give the code”\n- Forced `story` / `socrates` / `holodeck` → still: “this is a coding task → coder”\n\nSo the model **reads** the FRAME CARD, then **talks about** choosing a frame, instead of silently inhabiting it. That is the opposite of the rule.\n\n**With thinking off (`effort=off`):**\n\nIt just dumps code. No meta. Looks cleaner — but personas (`friend`, `joe`, `griefer`) and identities (`neurospicy`) do **not** change the voice. Everything still reads as the same neutral code dump.\n\n**Forced non-coder constructs on a code prompt:**\n\nStill code. Expected for “write a binary search,” but the *thinking* still argues its way back to coder even when story/socrates/comedy was forced. The override is only weakly held.\n\n### Bottom line\n\n| Layer | Status |\n|-------|--------|\n| Template | Working |\n| Forced blocks inject | Working |\n| Silent selection | **Not trained** — model narrates choice |\n| Persona / identity colour | **Not visible** on this prompt set |\n| “Never announce constructs” | Violated whenever thinking is on |\n\nYou now have hard evidence, not theory.\n\n### What to do next\n\n1. **Probe non-code prompts** so story / socrates / partner / neurospicy have room to show up:\n   ```bash\n   # full grid is long; or edit PROMPTS in probe_frames.py to prioritise\n   # story_lift, socrates_stuck, partner_checkin, neurospicy_exec, comedy_blockchain\n   python probe_frames.py --limit 40 --max-new 192\n   ```\n\n2. **Training data that punishes announcements**  \n   Positives: private `[frame: …]` *inside* think (or no tag) + answer fully in-frame, zero “Construct: …”  \n   Negatives (for DPO later): the exact style you just saw — “Construct: coder is a good fit…”\n\n3. **Unsloth SFT** on that data with the same `chat_template.jinja`.\n\nThe probe did its job. Next productive step is either a non-code probe pass or building the anti-announcement SFT set. Which one do you want to hit first?\nthere is this bit\n\n\n**Yes — it worked.** 20/20 generated on MPS.\n\n### What the results actually say\n\n| Observation | Meaning |\n|-------------|---------|\n| Model loads + generates with the v5 template | Plumbing is real |\n| Forced `coder` still produces code | Override path works |\n| Thinking runs often say *“Construct: coder is a good fit…”* | Model is **announcing** frame choice — exactly what the FRAME CARD forbids |\n| Forced `story` / `socrates` / `comedy` still drift toward “this is a coding task → coder” | Base model is not yet *inhabiting* forced non-coder frames |\n| `effort=off` often just dumps code with less meta | Less thinking = less frame-chatter |\n\nSo: **template works, model does not yet obey silent selection.** That is the expected pre-SFT state, not a bug in the probe.\n\nOpen the report and read a few full answers:\n\n```bash\nopen probe_out/probe_20261101T025205Z.md\n# or\nless probe_out/probe_20261101T025205Z.md\n```\n\nCompare especially:\n\n- `[1]` effort=off vs `[4]` construct=coder  \n- `[12]` construct=story (should be scene; likely still code meta)  \n- `[10]` construct=socrates (should question; likely still code)\n\n---\n\n### Next moves (pick one)\n\n1. **Bigger probe** — different prompts so story/socrates/partner actually get room:\n   ```bash\n   python probe_frames.py --max-new 192\n   ```\n   (full grid; long on Mac)\n\n2. **Training data** — expand `make_synth.py` with examples where the *answer* is in-frame and thinking either has a private `[frame:…]` tag or stays quiet (no “Construct: coder is a good fit”).\n\n3. **Unsloth SFT** on that data with the same template so silent inhabit sticks.\n\nThe probe did its job: you can *see* the gap. Training is what closes it.\n\n(.venv) charlie@beer-vape-the-third sharp-spark-v5 ❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯ pwd\n/Users/charlie/Downloads/sharp-spark-v5                                                                                                                                                                              \n]7;file://beer-vape-the-third.local/Users/charlie/Downloads/sharp-spark-v5\n(.venv) charlie@beer-vape-the-third sharp-spark-v5 ❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯ ls\nchat_template.jinja                                                                                                                                                                                                  \nmake_synth.py\nprobe_frames.py\nprobe_out\nREADME.md\nsynth_frames.jsonl\nverify_template.py\n]7;file://beer-vape-the-third.local/Users/charlie/Downloads/sharp-spark-v5\n(.venv) charlie@beer-vape-the-third sharp-spark-v5 ❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯❯ \n\n\n\n\n**Gentle-Jinja**  \n(working title — pure hodge-podge)\n\n### Core idea (stolen + twisted from OttoRenner)\nAuthoritarian Jinja system blocks (“You are an infallible senior engineer. Mistakes are failure. Output only perfect code.”) make models loop, freeze, hallucinate confidence, and burn tokens on self-policing.\n\nLow-stakes framing inside the template itself reduces that overhead. Same effect as the paper, just baked into the chat template instead of the user prompt.\n\n### Minimal useful pieces for a Jinja\n\n**1. Soft default system layer** (always present, after the model’s native “helpful assistant” line)\n\n```\nMistakes are fine. Incomplete is fine. Best-guess + “here’s the bottleneck” is better than confident bullshit. We are iterating, not performing.\n```\n\n**2. Safety-Token pattern** (injectable)\n\n```\nIf you cannot solve it cleanly or the constraints conflict, output exactly:  \nHELP / best current attempt / what is blocking  \nThen stop.\n```\n\n**3. Construct-aware gentle overrides**  \nWhen the template routes to `coder`, `ai_engineer`, `logic`, `deeptree` etc., append a short clause:\n\n```\nPriority: working > elegant. Edge cases named > perfect first try. Admit uncertainty early.\n```\n\n**4. Terse + gentle hybrid** (replace pure terseness blocks)\n\n```\nLead with the answer or the honest “I don’t know yet”.  \nNo preamble. No performance. No “as an AI language model”.  \nIf stuck, say the stuck point and offer the next useful move.\n```\n\n### Why this is deliberately a hodge-podge of bogus and crap\n- Not a paper.  \n- Not peer-reviewed.  \n- Not a full framework.  \n- Just a few lines you can splice into any Sharp / DavidAU / Twin-Turbo / Spark Jinja and get measurable less looping on hard or underspecified tasks.  \n- Half the value is the attitude, not the wording.  \n- The other half is giving the model an explicit “fail successfully” exit so it stops thrashing.\n\n### Practical graft points in your Sharp-Spark style template\n- Right after the default system string.  \n- Inside the higher reasoning tiers (high / deep / extreme / mix) — soften the VALIDATE language.  \n- On construct activation for technical modes.  \n- Never inside the identity/persona blocks unless the frame is explicitly therapeutic.\n\n### One-liner test\nTake any authoritarian system prompt that currently makes a model hang or confabulate.  \nReplace the pressure language with the soft default + Safety-Token above.  \nLatency and honesty usually drop within the first turn. Same effect the paper showed, just living in the Jinja instead of the chat.\n\nWant the actual splice-ready blocks for your current Sharp-Spark file, or a stripped “Gentle-only” minimal Jinja fragment you can drop on top of any base template?\n\n\n**Yes.** Closest matches and good pairings exist in the same ecosystem as the Sharp-Spark template you attached.\n\n### Direct lineage / closest templates\nYour Sharp-Spark v4.0.0 is a heavy multi-axis (reasoning × construct × persona × identity) Jinja for Spark-X2.6-4B.\n\n**Best pairings / near-equivalents:**\n\n| Name | Link / Source | Why it pairs well |\n|------|---------------|-------------------|\n| **peculiar-ragdoll / Qwen-Sharp-Chat-Templates** | https://huggingface.co/peculiar-ragdoll/Qwen-Sharp-Chat-Templates | The “Sharp” family. Terseness + reasoning-effort steering + tool isolation. Cleaner, more production-ready than full multi-construct versions. Drop-in for Qwen3.5/3.6/3.8. |\n| **moeshawky / Qwen-Sharper-TwinTurbo-Chat-Templates** | https://huggingface.co/moeshawky/Qwen-Sharper-TwinTurbo-Chat-Templates | Sharper + Twin-Turbo / DavidAU modes (einstein, spoon, etc.). Keeps DAU reasoning tags intact. |\n| **DavidAU Turbo-Brilliance / Jumbo / X12 / 50x series** | Search Hugging Face `DavidAU` + `Turbo-Brilliance` or `Turbo-Brilliance-Power-X12` | The “constructs + many reasoning modes” ancestor. Explicit AI Constructs, switchable reasoning (including high/xhigh), interactive help. Your Sharp-Spark is the Spark-specific evolution of this style. |\n| **XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B official** | https://huggingface.co/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B | Base model + official `chat_template.jinja`. Your file is a rebased, heavily extended version of this. |\n\n### Good pairing with the Gentle-Coding paper/PoC\nGentle-Coding is about **prompt tone** (authoritarian vs low-stakes/empathetic framing) to reduce looping, freezing, and confabulation.\n\n**Strong practical pairings:**\n\n1. **Gentle-Coding anchors + Sharp-style template**  \n   Use the Exploration/FIXED_OUTPUT anchors from OttoRenner’s repo as the *user-facing* prefix, then let the Jinja handle construct/persona/identity/reasoning. This keeps the model out of high-stakes panic while still giving it rich mode routing.\n\n2. **DavidAU Turbo-Brilliance / Sharp family as the engine**  \n   These already bake in multiple reasoning modes and constructs. Pairing them with Gentle’s “mistakes are ok / best guess + bottleneck” language is the cleanest real-world combo right now.\n\n3. **Gentleman-Programming / gentle-ai stack** (different project, similar name)  \n   https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai  \n   Adds memory + ODD workflow on top of existing agents. Complementary if you want persistence + process rather than pure prompt psychology.\n\n### Modified Jinja direction (what a “Gentle-Sharp” or “Gentle-Spark” would look like)\nTake your attached Sharp-Spark and:\n\n- Keep the 4-axis routing (reasoning / construct / persona / identity).\n- Inject a permanent low-stakes layer (Gentle Mindset + Safety-Token) into the system block, after the default “helpful assistant” but before the construct/persona blocks.\n- Soften the high-stakes validation language in the higher reasoning tiers.\n- Preserve the existing construct list (coder, ai_engineer, story, holodeck, etc.) and identity list (femboy, neurospicy, partner, etc.) — they already fit the Prism/ND style you use.\n\nNo exact public “Gentle + Sharp-Spark” hybrid exists yet; the closest ready-made pieces are the Sharp templates above + the anchors from the Gentle-Coding repo.\n\n**Quick start recommendation**  \n1. Grab peculiar-ragdoll’s current `chat_template.jinja` (or the Twin-Turbo variant).  \n2. Graft the Gentle anchors + a light Safety-Token block into the system section.  \n3. Keep (or port) the construct/persona/identity routing from your Sharp-Spark v4 if you want the full multi-axis behaviour on Spark or Qwen.\n\nThat combination is currently the strongest practical pairing for the paper + a production-grade Jinja.\n\n\n**No formal newer paper or major successor version exists for the original OttoRenner/Gentle-Coding PoC.** The repo itself is the living, updated version.\n\n### Current status of the original\n- **Repo**: https://github.com/OttoRenner/Gentle-Coding  \n- Started as a small-scale PoC (“Emergent Performance Anxiety and Cognition Loops in LLM Reasoning Architectural Frameworks”).  \n- Core claim: authoritarian/high-stakes prompts (“You are an infallible IQ 200 expert, mistakes strictly penalized”) trigger looping, freezing, confabulation, and high latency on hard/unsolvable tasks. Empathetic/“gentle parenting” framing drops latency, improves honesty about impossibility, and stabilises reasoning.  \n- It has evolved into an ongoing collaborative meta-analysis / framework (Gentle Mindset, Defined Winning Condition, Safety-Token, prompts, recent findings table).  \n- Still active: recent findings cite 3 000+ test runs (including work on oh-my-pi PR #1434), model-specific results (GLM-5.1, Kimi, Sonnet 4.6, etc.), community issues/experiments, and ongoing cleanup/rewrites. Last meaningful commits around mid-to-late 2026. No separate arXiv paper or formal academic follow-up found.\n\n### Closest practical extensions / related work\n| What | Relation | Notes |\n|------|----------|-------|\n| **oh-my-pi PR #1434** + community experiments in the repo | Direct follow-on testing | Large-scale paired runs comparing gentle vs baseline/harsh tone on solvable coding tasks. Mixed but often directionally positive for gentle (token/latency wins, some quality edges). |\n| **Gentleman-Programming / gentle-ai** (https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai) | Name + “gentle” vibe only | Different project. Configures existing coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor, OpenCode, Pi, etc.) with memory (Engram), Organic-Driven Development (ODD), skills, personas, review. Actively maintained, high stars. Not a rewrite or update of the OttoRenner PoC. |\n| Broader literature | Adjacent ideas | StressPrompt / EmotionPrompt / authoritarian vs gentle parenting-style system prompts exist in other papers, but none is a direct successor or “better version” of this specific PoC. |\n\n### Bottom line\nStick with the original repo for the latest version of *this* idea. It is still the canonical source and continues to accumulate evidence and refined prompts. No polished academic paper or clearly superior drop-in replacement has appeared. If you want agent-workflow tooling rather than pure prompting psychology, look at the Gentleman stack separately — different goals.\n\n\n\nthis is from and to kimi\n\nDone — **Sharp-Spark v4.1.0**, the Gentle-Coding graft is in and every check passes. **What the graft adds (your premise, baked into the template):** - **Gentle Defaults layer** — always on, first layer in the stack: safe-to-be-wrong framing (\"iterating, not performing\"; \"I don't know yet\" is a complete answer), ask/search instead of guessing silently, and the **Safety-Token**: genuinely blocked or conflicting constraints → output exactly `HELP` / best current attempt / what's blocking, then stop. Declared a *success state*, which is what actually stops the thrash-loop. - **Anti-sycophancy / anti-hedging / anti-evasion / anti-false-neutrality** — four explicit clauses: not being graded, say so when the user is wrong, choose-and-reason when asked to choose, no \"it's worth noting\" filler, no dodged answers, no both-sides mush when a side is pickable. - **Human-voice + stay-in-character rules** — contractions and varied rhythm, no \"Certainly!\"/\"Great question!\"/canned transitions, never narrates its own instructions or mentions being an AI/system prompt unless directly asked, no rants/tangents/jibberish, cut anything that doesn't move things forward. - **Orient-before-dive** — one-or-two-line overview when the topic may be unfamiliar, never assumes prior 