g boy and age regression parts to it also we can go up to 50 odd constructs personas identities as well so I think it’s best we split it up so the chat or whatever has 4 things to determine it 1 reasoning type 10 choices 2 constructs (dictates the chat session) 3 personas (how it acts) 4 identity (who and what it thinks it is) and maybe add an llm/mla/cloud compute engineer and top dawg and maybe some Lolita and Bonita and an anarchist / griefer / hacker / espionage / spy / imposter thing\n\nDone — v2.5.1, addressing every point in the review. All 8 test groups pass under a strict minja-style environment (indent-only `tojson`, no `fromjson`, no reliance on `loop.previtem`).\n\n**🔴 The crash — fixed.** Both `tojson(separators=...)` calls stripped to plain `tojson`. Verified rendering tool definitions and dict-valued args under a simulated minja filter (`indent` parameter only) — no TypeError. Whitespace in tool JSON is indeed irrelevant to the model.\n\n**🟡 The two portability risks — fixed/defended:**\n1. `loop.previtem/nextitem` replaced with plain index arithmetic (`messages[loop.index0 ± 1]` + `if` guards). Same output — verified with both 2- and 3-message tool groups — but built from primitives minja definitely has. \n2. `raise_exception` — kept deliberately, and the header now states the requirement plainly (llama.cpp ✓, transformers ✓, vLLM ✓). If a runtime lacks it, the template fails *loudly at render*, not silently mid-generation. That's the correct failure mode.\n\n**🟠 String tool args — kept, with honest documentation.** The passthrough stays (the alternative is a hard crash), but the header now warns: one `<arg_value>` blob ≠ trained `arg_key/arg_value` pairs, so multi-arg string calls may degrade in quality. The documented fix is at the runtime layer — pre-parse arguments to a mapping before `apply_chat_template`, which most OpenAI-compatible servers already do.\n\n**⚙️ Tier precedence — documented** in the header exactly as the reviewer stated it: `ena