ional\n\n[](/models?other=custom_code)\n\ncustom\\_code\n\nLicense: apache-2.0\n\n[ Model card](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent) [ Files Files and versions](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent/tree/main)\n\nxet\n\n[ Community](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent/discussions)\n\nDeploy\n\nCopy to bucket\n\nUse this model\n\n### Instructions to use trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent with libraries, inference providers, notebooks, and local apps. Follow these links to get started.\n\n- Libraries\n\n- [ Transformers](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent?library=transformers)\n\n  How to use trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent with Transformers:\n\n```\n# Use a pipeline as a high-level helper\nfrom transformers import pipeline\n\npipe = pipeline(\"text-generation\", model=\"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\", trust_remote_code=True)\nmessages = [\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Who are you?\"},\n]\npipe(messages)\n```\n\n```\n# pip install -U transformers accelerate\n# Load model directly\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\", trust_remote_code=True, device_map=\"auto\")\n```\n\n- Notebooks\n\n- [ Google Colab](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent/colab)\n\n- [ Kaggle](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent/kaggle)\n\n- Local Apps [Settings](/settings/local-apps \"Set up your favorite local applications\")\n\n- [ vLLM](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent?local-app=vllm)\n\n  How to use trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent with vLLM:\n\n  ##### Install from pip and serve model\n\n```\n# Install vLLM from pip:\npip install vllm\n# Start the vLLM server:\nvllm serve \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\"\n# Call the server using curl (OpenAI-compatible API):\ncurl -X POST \"http://localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions\" \\\n\t-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n\t--data '{\n\t\t\"model\": \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\",\n\t\t\"messages\": [\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\"role\": \"user\",\n\t\t\t\t\"content\": \"What is the capital of France?\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t]\n\t}'\n```\n\n  ##### Use Docker\n\n```\ndocker model run hf.co/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\n```\n\n- [ SGLang](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent?local-app=sglang)\n\n  How to use trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent with SGLang:\n\n  ##### Install from pip and serve model\n\n```\n# Install SGLang from pip:\npip install sglang\n# Start the SGLang server:\npython3 -m sglang.launch_server \\\n    --model-path \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\" \\\n    --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n    --port 30000\n# Call the server using curl (OpenAI-compatible API):\ncurl -X POST \"http://localhost:30000/v1/chat/completions\" \\\n\t-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n\t--data '{\n\t\t\"model\": \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\",\n\t\t\"messages\": [\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\"role\": \"user\",\n\t\t\t\t\"content\": \"What is the capital of France?\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t]\n\t}'\n```\n\n  ##### Use Docker images\n\n```\ndocker run --gpus all \\\n    --shm-size 32g \\\n    -p 30000:30000 \\\n    -v ~/.cache/huggingface:/root/.cache/huggingface \\\n    --env \"HF_TOKEN=<secret>\" \\\n    --ipc=host \\\n    lmsysorg/sglang:latest \\\n    python3 -m sglang.launch_server \\\n        --model-path \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\" \\\n        --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n        --port 30000\n# Call the server using curl (OpenAI-compatible API):\ncurl -X POST \"http://localhost:30000/v1/chat/completions\" \\\n\t-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n\t--data '{\n\t\t\"model\": \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\",\n\t\t\"messages\": [\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\"role\": \"user\",\n\t\t\t\t\"content\": \"What is the capital of France?\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t]\n\t}'\n```\n\n- [ Docker Model Runner](/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent?local-app=docker-model-runner)\n\n  How to use trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent with Docker Model Runner:\n\n```\ndocker model run hf.co/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\n```\n\n-\n\n  [Browse Quantizations](/models?other=base_model:quantized:trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent) to use this model in llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio, or any compatible app.\n\n- [Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent](#spark-x26-4b-sql-cypher-agent \"Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\")\n\n  - [Results](#results \"Results\")\n\n  - [How to use it](#how-to-use-it \"How to use it\")\n\n  - [Training](#training \"Training\")\n\n  - [Limitations](#limitations \"Limitations\")\n\n  - [Licence and attribution](#licence-and-attribution \"Licence and attribution\")\n\n# [](#spark-x26-4b-sql-cypher-agent) Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\n\nA 4B tool-calling agent that answers questions about a database by writing **one read-only query**: SQLite SQL against a relational schema or Neo4j Cypher against a graph schema. It thinks first, then calls whatever query tool you give it. It's a LoRA fine-tune of [XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](https://huggingface.co/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B), merged into the base weights, trained on a single 16 GB consumer GPU (AMD RX 7800 XT).\n\n- **This repo:** the merged model in bf16 (Transformers, `trust_remote_code=True`).\n\n- **GGUF** for llama.cpp (Q8\\_0, Q6\\_K\\_XL, Q6\\_K, Q4\\_K\\_XL, Q4\\_K\\_M): [trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent-GGUF](https://huggingface.co/trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent-GGUF)\n\n## [](#results) Results\n\n**Execution accuracy** on a held-out test set of 1,000 questions, 250 from each dataset's own test split. None of the questions appear in training. The same harness ran both models: llama.cpp, the same prompts, greedy decoding, a thinking budget of 1,024 tokens.\n\n| Model | Overall | CypherBench | gretel | SQaLe | text2cypher | Well-formed call | Query parses |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Spark-X2.6-4B (base, Q8\\_0) | 32\\.5% | 22\\.4% | 55\\.3% | 36\\.6% | 12\\.4% | 95\\.3% | 92\\.2% |\n| **This model (Q8\\_0)** | **43\\.7%** | **35\\.5%** | **64\\.6%** | **43\\.2%** | **27\\.7%** | **97\\.8%** | **95\\.8%** |\n\n**\\+10.7 points overall** (95% interval ±2.9). Compared question by question on the 856 questions both runs could score: the fine-tune alone got 129 right, the base alone got 37 right (sign test p ≈ 4e-13). The unmerged adapter on the bf16 base scored 43\\.9% on the same test, so merging and quantising to Q8\\_0 cost nothing measurable.\n\n**How an answer is scored.** A question counts only if the query returns the same result as the dataset's reference query, not if it merely looks similar:\n\n- **gretel:** both queries run on the example's real rows.\n\n- **SQaLe:** both run on several randomly filled copies of the schema. This is the test-suite idea from Spider: two queries that agree on several random databases very likely mean the same thing. Some copies place values right next to the query's literals, so `>` against `>=` or `LIKE` against `=` give different rows.\n\n- **CypherBench:** both run on the dataset's own sampled graphs in Neo4j.\n\n- **text2cypher:** both run on random graphs built from the prompt's schema.\n\nResults are compared as multisets of rows, and row order counts when the reference sorts. A question where no database tells the two queries apart is left out, which is why the scored counts are just under 1,000. Exact-match scores would be far lower and mean little here.\n\n## [](#how-to-use-it) How to use it\n\nThe model expects the setup it was trained on:\n\n- a **system message** with the schema, optional example queries and a few rules;\n\n- the **question** as the user message;\n\n- one **query tool** in `tools`.\n\nThe tool's name and argument don't matter (training used `run_query(query)`, `execute_sql(sql)`, `cypher_query(cypher)`, ...), and unrelated tools in the list are ignored. If the schema can't answer the question, the model is trained to say so in one sentence instead of calling the tool.\n\n~~~python\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nrepo = \"trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(repo, trust_remote_code=True)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(repo, trust_remote_code=True, torch_dtype=\"bfloat16\",\n                                             device_map=\"auto\")\n\nschema = \"\"\"CREATE TABLE teams (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, city TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE games (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, season INTEGER, home_id INTEGER, away_id INTEGER,\n                    home_score INTEGER, away_score INTEGER);\"\"\"\nsystem = f\"\"\"You answer questions about the database below by calling the `run_query` tool with ONE read-only SQLite SQL query.\n\n{schema}\n\nExamples (tested queries on this database):\n(none)\n\nRules:\n- One statement, read only (SELECT or WITH ... SELECT). No comments. The query runs as written, so use literal values, not parameters.\n- Return named columns: the names and the numbers the answer needs. Never return whole rows of every column unless the question asks for them.\n- Match names and values as the data spells them.\n- Return exactly the rows the question asks for, in its shape: one row for a single answer (\"which is the most\", \"the highest\": ORDER BY the measure, LIMIT 1); one row per entity for \"which ...\", \"every\", \"list\"; the top N for \"top N\".\n- If the database can't answer the question, don't call the tool: say why in one short sentence.\"\"\"\ntools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"run_query\",\n    \"description\": \"Run one read-only SQLite SQL query against the database and return the result rows.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The query text.\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"query\"]}}}]\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": system},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Which team scored the most points at home in 2024?\"}]\n\nids = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tools=tools, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device)\nout = model.generate(ids, max_new_tokens=2048, do_sample=False)\nprint(tok.decode(out[0, ids.shape[1]:]))\n# ... thought ...</think><tool_call>run_query<arg_key>query</arg_key><arg_value>SELECT ...</arg_value></tool_call>\n~~~\n\nFor a graph, use the same shape with \"graph database\", \"Neo4j Cypher\", `MATCH ... RETURN` and \"nodes\", and give the schema as node labels, relationship types and their properties.\n\n**Thinking** is on by default (`enable_thinking=True`, `reasoning_effort=\"high\"`; `\"low\"` also works). Pass `enable_thinking=False` to `apply_chat_template` for a direct call. The scores above use thinking with a 1,024-token budget; the median thought is about 650 characters.\n\n**Decoding.** Every score above uses greedy decoding (`do_sample=False`, or `temperature=0` on a server). `generation_config.json` is the base model's sampling default (temperature 1\\.0, top-p 0\\.95), which suits chat better than query writing.\n\n**Serving.** For llama.cpp, use the GGUF repo. It needs llama.cpp **b10828 or newer** for the Spark architecture. `llama-server`'s OpenAI-compatible API returns the thought in `message.reasoning_content` and the call parsed in `message.tool_calls`. Pass thinking options as `\"chat_template_kwargs\": {\"enable_thinking\": false}` or `{\"reasoning_effort\": \"low\"}`. Spark's template accepts only `high` and `low`, so don't set the server-wide `--reasoning-effort medium`.\n\n## [](#training) Training\n\n- **Method:** LoRA, rank 16 / alpha 32 / dropout 0\\.05, on every linear layer of every block (`q_k_v_proj`, `out_proj`, `gate_proj`, `up_proj`, `down_proj`). AdamW, lr 1e-4, 3% warmup, cosine decay, weight decay 0\\.01, batch 32, max length 4,096, seed 42\\. The loss is on the assistant turn only.\n\n- **Data: 94,745 examples, about 66M tokens, one epoch.**\n\n  - **90,549 gold calls:** each dataset's reference query, written as a thinking-off tool call. SQaLe 27,887, gretel 28,695, text2cypher 25,871, CypherBench 8,096.\n\n  - **4,196 self-generated traces:** the base model's own thought and tool call on training questions, kept only when the query passed the execution checks above (thinking high 2,829 / low 1,129 / off 238). These teach it to reason before calling without drifting from its own style.\n\n- **Checkpoint chosen:** optimizer step **1,500 of 2,961**. A snapshot was scored every 500 steps on a separate 200-question evalset. The final adapter and the best mid-run one (step 1,500) were both run on the test set. Step 1,500 scored 43\\.9% against 42\\.2% for the final one, and both held-out sets pointed the same way, so step 1,500 shipped.\n\n- **Hardware:** one AMD Radeon RX 7800 XT (16 GB, ROCm) under WSL, about 24 hours for the run. The pipeline (trace generation, checking, training, evaluation) is a personal project.\n\n## [](#limitations) Limitations\n\n- **About 44% of answers return the reference result.** It's a 4B model. Run its queries read-only, and check them before trusting a number, especially on unfamiliar schemas.\n\n- **One query per answer, one turn.** It wasn't trained to read a tool result and try again (multi-step agent loops are the next stage of the project).\n\n- **Dialects:** trained on SQLite SQL and Neo4j Cypher. Other SQL dialects work as far as their syntax overlaps with SQLite.\n\n- **English only**, and questions in the style of the four datasets: single-database analytics, joins, groups, top-N, filters.\n\n- It sometimes adds `SELECT DISTINCT` where the reference doesn't, which changes row counts on duplicate-heavy tables.\n\n- text2cypher is the weakest set (27.7%); its databases aren't public, so its answers are checked on random graphs, a harder and noisier test.\n\n## [](#licence-and-attribution) Licence and attribution\n\nApache-2.0, like the base model. Base model: [XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](https://huggingface.co/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B) (Apache-2.0).\n\nTraining data, used under their licences:\n\n| Dataset | Licence |\n| --- | --- |\n| [trl-lab/SQaLe-2-text-to-SQL-Queries](https://huggingface.co/datasets/trl-lab/SQaLe-2-text-to-SQL-Queries) and its schemas | MIT |\n| [gretelai/synthetic\\_text\\_to\\_sql](https://huggingface.co/datasets/gretelai/synthetic_text_to_sql) | Apache-2.0 |\n| [neo4j/text2cypher-2025v1](https://huggingface.co/datasets/neo4j/text2cypher-2025v1) | Apache-2.0 |\n| [megagonlabs/cypherbench](https://huggingface.co/datasets/megagonlabs/cypherbench) | Apache-2.0 |\n\n**Downloads last month**\n: \\-\n\nSafetensors[](https://huggingface.co/docs/safetensors)\n\nModel size\n\n4B params\n\nTensor type\n\nBF16\n\n·\n\nChat template\n\nFiles info\n\nInference Providers [NEW](https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-providers)\n\n[](/tasks/text-generation \"Learn more about text-generation\")\n\nText Generation\n\nThis model isn't deployed by any Inference Provider. [🙋 Ask for provider support](/spaces/huggingface/InferenceSupport/discussions/new?title=trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent&description=React%20to%20this%20comment%20with%20an%20emoji%20to%20vote%20for%20%5Btrjxter%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent%5D\\(%2Ftrjxter%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\\)%20to%20be%20supported%20by%20Inference%20Providers.%0A%0A\\(optional\\)%20Which%20providers%20are%20you%20interested%20in%3F%20\\(Novita%2C%20Hyperbolic%2C%20Together%E2%80%A6\\)%0A)\n\n## Model tree for trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent [](/docs/hub/model-cards#specifying-a-base-model)\n\nBase model\n\n[XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B-Base](/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B-Base)\n\nFinetuned\n\n[XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B)\n\nFinetuned\n\n([17](/models?other=base_model:finetune:XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B))\n\nthis model\n\nQuantizations\n\n[1 model](/models?other=base_model:quantized:trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent)\n\n## Datasets used to train trjxter/Spark-X2.6-4B-SQL-Cypher-Agent\n\n[](/datasets/gret