LE3/discussions)\n\nDeploy\n\nCopy to bucket\n\nUse this model\n\n### Instructions to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with libraries, inference providers, notebooks, and local apps. Follow these links to get started.\n\n- Notebooks\n\n- [ Google Colab](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3/colab)\n\n- [ Kaggle](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3/kaggle)\n\n- Local Apps [Settings](/settings/local-apps \"Set up your favorite local applications\")\n\n- [ llama.cpp](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=llama.cpp)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with llama.cpp:\n\n  ##### Install (macOS, Linux)\n\n```\ncurl -LsSf https://llama.app/install.sh | sh\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server with a web UI:\nllama serve -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n# Run inference directly in the terminal:\nllama cli -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Install from WinGet (Windows)\n\n```\nwinget install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server with a web UI:\nllama serve -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n# Run inference directly in the terminal:\nllama cli -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Build from source code\n\n```\ngit clone https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp.git\ncd llama.cpp\ncmake -B build\ncmake --build build -j --target llama-app\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server with a web UI:\n./build/bin/llama serve -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n# Run inference directly in the terminal:\n./build/bin/llama cli -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Use Docker\n\n```\ndocker model run hf.co/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n- [ Llama new](llama://install?repo=VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3)\n\n- [ LM Studio](lmstudio://open_from_hf?model=VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3)\n\n- [ Jan](jan://models/huggingface/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3)\n\n- [ Ollama](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=ollama)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with Ollama:\n\n```\nollama run hf.co/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n- [ Unsloth Desktop](unsloth://open_from_hf?model=VitreousCut%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3)\n\n- [ Pi](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=pi)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with Pi:\n\n  ##### Start the llama.cpp server\n\n```\n# Install llama.cpp:\nbrew install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server:\nllama serve -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Configure the model in Pi\n\n```\n# Install Pi:\nnpm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n# Add to ~/.pi/agent/models.json:\n{\n  \"providers\": {\n    \"llama-cpp\": {\n      \"baseUrl\": \"http://localhost:8080/v1\",\n      \"api\": \"openai-completions\",\n      \"apiKey\": \"none\",\n      \"models\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n  ##### Run Pi\n\n```\n# Start Pi in your project directory:\npi\n```\n\n- [ Docker Model Runner](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=docker-model-runner)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with Docker Model Runner:\n\n```\ndocker model run hf.co/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n- [ Lemonade](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=lemonade)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with Lemonade:\n\n  ##### Pull the model\n\n```\n# Download Lemonade from https://lemonade-server.ai/\nlemonade pull VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Run and chat with the model\n\n```\nlemonade run user.Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3-{{QUANT_TAG}}\n```\n\n  ##### List all available models\n\n```\nlemonade list\n```\n\n- [ Hermes Agent](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=hermes-agent)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with Hermes Agent:\n\n  ##### Start the llama.cpp server\n\n```\n# Install llama.cpp:\nbrew install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server:\nllama serve -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Configure Hermes\n\n```\n# Install Hermes:\ncurl -fsSL https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.sh | bash\nhermes setup\n# Point Hermes at the local server:\nhermes config set model.provider custom\nhermes config set model.base_url http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\nhermes config set model.default VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Run Hermes\n\n```\nhermes\n```\n\n- [ Atomic Chat](atomic-chat://models/huggingface/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3)\n\n- [ OpenClaw](/VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3?local-app=openclaw)\n\n  How to use VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 with OpenClaw:\n\n  ##### Start the llama.cpp server\n\n```\n# Install llama.cpp:\nbrew install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server:\nllama serve -hf VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\n```\n\n  ##### Configure OpenClaw\n\n```\n# Install OpenClaw:\nnpm install -g openclaw@latest\n# Register the local server and set it as the default model:\nopenclaw onboard --non-interactive --mode local \\\n  --auth-choice custom-api-key \\\n  --custom-base-url http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1 \\\n  --custom-model-id \"VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\" \\\n  --custom-provider-id llama-cpp \\\n  --custom-compatibility openai \\\n  --custom-text-input \\\n  --accept-risk \\\n  --skip-health\n```\n\n  ##### Run OpenClaw\n\n```\nopenclaw agent --local --agent main --message \"Hello from Hugging Face\"\n```\n\n- [Spark-X2.6-4B EAGLE-3 draft head](#spark-x26-4b-eagle-3-draft-head \"Spark-X2.6-4B EAGLE-3 draft head\")\n\n  - [⚠️ Requires a one-line llama.cpp patch](#⚠️-requires-a-one-line-llamacpp-patch \"⚠️ Requires a one-line llama.cpp patch\")\n\n  - [Quick start](#quick-start \"Quick start\")\n\n  - [Specs](#specs \"Specs\")\n\n  - [Training](#training \"Training\")\n\n  - [Results](#results \"Results\")\n\n  - [Limitations](#limitations \"Limitations\")\n\n  - [Files](#files \"Files\")\n\n  - [License](#license \"License\")\n\n  - [Citation](#citation \"Citation\")\n\n# [](#spark-x26-4b-eagle-3-draft-head) Spark-X2.6-4B EAGLE-3 draft head\n\nA one-layer EAGLE-3 draft head (218M parameters) for [XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](https://huggingface.co/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B). It proposes tokens that the 4B model verifies, so speculative decoding runs faster while the output stays exactly what the target model would have produced.\n\nThere was no public draft head for Spark, so this is a from-scratch one. It is provided so you can combine it with whatever target GGUF you already use.\n\nThis is **v8**, the current release: trained on a mixed code \\+ prose \\+ conversation corpus with verifier-argmax distillation and the EAGLE-3 training-time test.\n\n## [](#⚠️-requires-a-one-line-llamacpp-patch) ⚠️ Requires a one-line llama.cpp patch\n\nllama.cpp's EAGLE-3 draft consumes the *input* to three target decoder layers, exposed per architecture as `res->t_layer_inp[il]`. Spark's arch (`spark2_5`) does not set it, and the runtime aborts with:\n\n```\nGGML_ASSERT(t_layer_inp[il] != nullptr)\n```\n\nApply `llama.cpp-spark2_5-layer-inputs.patch` (included here) to `src/models/spark2-5.cpp` and rebuild:\n\n```diff\n     for (int il = 0; il < n_layer; ++il) {\n+        res->t_layer_inp[il] = inpL;\n+\n         ggml_tensor * inpSA = inpL;\n```\n\nTested against llama.cpp `0.7.0-dev` (commit `f5027fb`).\n\n## [](#quick-start) Quick start\n\n```bash\nllama-server \\\n    --model Spark-X2.6-4B-Q8_0.gguf \\\n    --draft spark-eagle3-v8.gguf --spec-type draft-eagle3 \\\n    --spec-draft-n-max 3 \\\n    -ngl 99 --ngld 99\n```\n\nWhere the 4B target does not fit in VRAM, keep it on the CPU and put only the head on the GPU — the head costs \\~0.5 GiB including a 4k context:\n\n```bash\nllama-server \\\n    --model Spark-X2.6-4B-Q8_0.gguf \\\n    --draft spark-eagle3-v8.gguf --spec-type draft-eagle3 \\\n    --spec-draft-n-max 3 \\\n    --ngl 0 --ngld 99 --threads 4 -c 4096 -np 1\n```\n\n**`--spec-draft-n-max`:** `3` for prose, `2` never, `1` for code. Prose depth-1 acceptance is 0\\.75, so a pass returning \\~3.2 tokens beats verifying four; code depth-1 is \\~0.15, so only n=1 helps there.\n\n**Avoid `2` when the target is on the CPU.** It is not a middle setting: in llama.cpp 0\\.7.0-dev a pass at `n_max 2` costs \\~350 ms against \\~182 ms at `1` and \\~217 ms at `3`, so it lands below both (prose 1\\.28x vs 1\\.84x/2.57x). The same value behaves normally with the target in VRAM, so this is a CPU-side code-path artifact.\n\n## [](#specs) Specs\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Architecture | EAGLE-3, 1 decoder layer |\n| Parameters | 218M (32k draft vocab dominates) |\n| Base model | `XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B` (Apache-2.0) |\n| Target layers fused | 2, 18, 33 |\n| Hidden size | 2560 |\n| Attention | 16 heads / 4 KV, head\\_dim 256, RoPE theta 1e6 |\n| Draft vocab | 32,000 with `d2t` / `t2d` maps |\n| Weight file | 422 MB bf16 GGUF, 834 MB fp32 safetensors |\n\nNote for anyone loading the safetensors by hand: `d2t` is stored as **deltas** (`convert_hf_to_gguf.py` adds `arange` to recover absolute ids). Exporting absolute ids fails with `EAGLE-3 d2t target ids out of range for target vocab size 131072`.\n\n## [](#training) Training\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Corpus | 21\\.96M tokens: 10M Python repo code (codeparrot-clean), 3M instruction→code (Magicoder), 2M prose (WikiText-103), 7M conversation (UltraChat-200k, 32% of segments) |\n| Steps | 5,362 (one epoch, batch 4 × 1024) |\n| Objective | CE against the **target's own argmax**, plus 0\\.5 · MSE on the fed-back prenorm state, plus a depth-1 CE from the **training-time test** |\n| Hardware | single RTX 3090 at 300 W, target frozen in bf16 |\n\nThree choices carried the gain, each measured against the previous version:\n\n**Label on the verifier, not the corpus.** Acceptance is `draft argmax == target argmax`, so wherever the corpus token is not what Spark would emit, ordinary cross-entropy teaches a position the draft can never win. Relabelling lifted prose acceptance 0\\.49 → 0\\.81 at identical cost.\n\n**Training-time test.** The head is also fed its own prenorm output back as the next step's `g`, with a second loss there, so it learns to recover from its own drafts. This raised prose depth-1 acceptance 0\\.50 → 0\\.70 and is what makes `n_max > 1` pay.\n\n**Conversational data.** Instruct prose rendered through Spark's own template lifted chat-format acceptance 0\\.299 → 0\\.325 (0.377 with thinking disabled). Templated *code* in the same quantity moved it 0\\.001, so the win is the register, not the markers.\n\n## [](#results) Results\n\nDepth-0 acceptance, greedy, 96-token generations, target in VRAM:\n\n| prompt form | p0 |\n| --- | --- |\n| WikiText prose (in-domain) | 0\\.920 |\n| 12 mixed prompts, raw text | 0\\.464 |\n| 12 mixed prompts, chat template (thinking on) | 0\\.325 |\n| 3 Python code prompts | 0\\.415 |\n\nThroughput, single RTX 3090 \\+ i7-6700K (n=3 unless noted):\n\n| placement | prose | mixed | chat format | code |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Q8 target on CPU (5.79 tok/s), head on GPU | **2\\.57x** | 1\\.48x (n=1) | 1\\.24x (n=1) | – |\n| Q8 target in VRAM (\\~140 tok/s), head on GPU | 2\\.04x | 1\\.18x (n=1) | 1\\.06x (n=1) | 1\\.08x (n=1) |\n\nAt n=1 the CPU placement gives 1\\.88x on prose and 1\\.48x on mixed prompts. The gain scales with how slow the verifier is: with the target in VRAM a token costs \\~7 ms, so the draft's fixed per-pass cost stops being negligible.\n\n## [](#limitations) Limitations\n\n- **Chat format is still weaker than raw text.** 0\\.325 against 0\\.464 on the same prompts: the template opens a thinking channel, and the corpus contains no reasoning traces, so thinking-mode acceptance lags thinking-off (0.377).\n\n- **Narrow out of domain.** 0\\.92 in-domain but 0\\.46 on mixed prompts. A public EAGLE-3 head for Qwen3-4B scored 0\\.53–0.64 on the same mixed/code prompt sets.\n\n- **Code is the weakest domain**, and p1 ≈ 0\\.15 means only `n_max 1` is useful. Code p0 is 0\\.415 here against 0\\.437 for the previous release; that difference is **within the ±0.03 noise of a 3-prompt evaluation** and coincided with cutting the code share from 12M to 10M tokens, so treat it as unresolved rather than real.\n\n- **Two-layer capacity is blocked upstream**: `convert_hf_to_gguf.py` only detects EAGLE-3 at `num_hidden_layers == 1`, and llama.cpp's `eagle3` graph builds one layer, so a deeper head needs patches on both sides.\n\n- **Small evaluation.** 12 hand-written \\+ 12 WikiText \\+ 3 code prompts, in two prompt forms; the mixed and code figures carry roughly ±0.03 uncertainty.\n\n- **Spark only.** The head reads Spark's hidden states at layers 2/18/33 with Spark's tokenizer; it will not work with another model.\n\n## [](#files) Files\n\n| file | purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `spark-eagle3-v8.gguf` | current release, `--spec-type draft-eagle3` |\n| `v8/model.safetensors`, `v8/config.json` | v8 fp32 HF-format checkpoint |\n| `v8/train_log.json` | v8 training curve |\n| `llama.cpp-spark2_5-layer-inputs.patch` | required for the draft to load at all |\n| `spark-eagle3-v6.gguf`, `v6/…` | previous release, kept for existing links |\n| `spark-eagle3-v5.gguf`, root `model.safetensors`/`config.json` | first release, kept |\n\nTo rebuild the GGUF from the safetensors (needs a local copy of the Spark config and tokenizer, which the converter reads but does not instantiate):\n\n```bash\npython3 convert_hf_to_gguf.py v8 --target-model-dir /path/to/Spark-X2.6-4B \\\n    --outfile spark-eagle3-v8.gguf --outtype bf16\n```\n\n## [](#license) License\n\nApache-2.0, following the base model. Training data: codeparrot-clean (permissively licensed GitHub Python), Magicoder OSS-Instruct (MIT), UltraChat-200k (MIT), WikiText-103 (CC BY-SA 3\\.0) — the last is share-alike, which is worth knowing if you redistribute this commercially.\n\n## [](#citation) Citation\n\nIf this is useful, cite the EAGLE-3 paper for the architecture (Li et al., 2025) and link back here for the weights.\n\n**Downloads last month**\n: 1,056\n\nSafetensors[](https://huggingface.co/docs/safetensors)\n\nModel size\n\n0\\.2B params\n\nTensor type\n\nI64\n\n·\n\nF32\n\n·\n\nFiles info\n\nInference Providers [NEW](https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-providers)\n\nThis model isn't deployed by any Inference Provider. [🙋 Ask for provider support](/spaces/huggingface/InferenceSupport/discussions/new?title=VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3&description=React%20to%20this%20comment%20with%20an%20emoji%20to%20vote%20for%20%5BVitreousCut%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3%5D\\(%2FVitreousCut%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3\\)%20to%20be%20supported%20by%20Inference%20Providers.%0A%0A\\(optional\\)%20Which%20providers%20are%20you%20interested%20in%3F%20\\(Novita%2C%20Hyperbolic%2C%20Together%E2%80%A6\\)%0A)\n\n## Model tree for VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3 [](/docs/hub/model-cards#specifying-a-base-model)\n\nBase model\n\n[XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B-Base](/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B-Base)\n\nFinetuned\n\n[XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B)\n\nQuantized\n\n([52](/models?other=base_model:quantized:XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B))\n\nthis model\n\nFinetunes\n\n[1 model](/models?other=base_model:finetune:VitreousCut/Spark-X2.6-4B-EAGLE3)\n\nSystem theme\n\nCompany\n\n[TOS](/terms-of-service) [Privacy](/privacy) [About](/huggingface) [Careers](https://apply.workable.com/huggingface/) [](/)\n\nWebsite\n\n[Models](/models) [Datasets](/datasets) [Spaces](/spaces) [Pricing](/pricing) [Docs](/docs)"
