el\n\n```\n# Install vLLM from pip:\npip install vllm\n# Start the vLLM server:\nvllm serve \"darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED\"\n# Call the server using curl (OpenAI-compatible API):\ncurl -X POST \"http://localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions\" \\\n\t-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n\t--data '{\n\t\t\"model\": \"darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED\",\n\t\t\"messages\": [\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\"role\": \"user\",\n\t\t\t\t\"content\": \"What is the capital of France?\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t]\n\t}'\n```\n\n  ##### Use Docker\n\n```\ndocker model run hf.co/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n- [ Ollama](/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED?local-app=ollama)\n\n  How to use darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED with Ollama:\n\n```\nollama run hf.co/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n- [ Unsloth Desktop](unsloth://open_from_hf?model=darioooooo0o%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-CED)\n\n- [ Pi](/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED?local-app=pi)\n\n  How to use darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED with Pi:\n\n  ##### Start the llama.cpp server\n\n```\n# Install llama.cpp:\nbrew install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server:\nllama serve -hf darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n  ##### Configure the model in Pi\n\n```\n# Install Pi:\nnpm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n# Add to ~/.pi/agent/models.json:\n{\n  \"providers\": {\n    \"llama-cpp\": {\n      \"baseUrl\": \"http://localhost:8080/v1\",\n      \"api\": \"openai-completions\",\n      \"apiKey\": \"none\",\n      \"models\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n  ##### Run Pi\n\n```\n# Start Pi in your project directory:\npi\n```\n\n- [ Docker Model Runner](/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED?local-app=docker-model-runner)\n\n  How to use darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED with Docker Model Runner:\n\n```\ndocker model run hf.co/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n- [ Lemonade](/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED?local-app=lemonade)\n\n  How to use darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED with Lemonade:\n\n  ##### Pull the model\n\n```\n# Download Lemonade from https://lemonade-server.ai/\nlemonade pull darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n  ##### Run and chat with the model\n\n```\nlemonade run user.Spark-X2.6-4B-CED-Q4_K_M\n```\n\n  ##### List all available models\n\n```\nlemonade list\n```\n\n- [ Hermes Agent](/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED?local-app=hermes-agent)\n\n  How to use darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED with Hermes Agent:\n\n  ##### Start the llama.cpp server\n\n```\n# Install llama.cpp:\nbrew install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server:\nllama serve -hf darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n  ##### Configure Hermes\n\n```\n# Install Hermes:\ncurl -fsSL https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.sh | bash\nhermes setup\n# Point Hermes at the local server:\nhermes config set model.provider custom\nhermes config set model.base_url http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\nhermes config set model.default darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n  ##### Run Hermes\n\n```\nhermes\n```\n\n- [ Atomic Chat](atomic-chat://models/huggingface/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED)\n\n- [ OpenClaw](/darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED?local-app=openclaw)\n\n  How to use darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED with OpenClaw:\n\n  ##### Start the llama.cpp server\n\n```\n# Install llama.cpp:\nbrew install llama.cpp\n# Start a local OpenAI-compatible server:\nllama serve -hf darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\n```\n\n  ##### Configure OpenClaw\n\n```\n# Install OpenClaw:\nnpm install -g openclaw@latest\n# Register the local server and set it as the default model:\nopenclaw onboard --non-interactive --mode local \\\n  --auth-choice custom-api-key \\\n  --custom-base-url http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1 \\\n  --custom-model-id \"darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED:Q4_K_M\" \\\n  --custom-provider-id llama-cpp \\\n  --custom-compatibility openai \\\n  --custom-text-input \\\n  --accept-risk \\\n  --skip-health\n```\n\n  ##### Run OpenClaw\n\n```\nopenclaw agent --local --agent main --message \"Hello from Hugging Face\"\n```\n\n- [Spark-X2.6-4B with a Causal Encoder-Decoder (CED) split: experimental retrofit](#spark-x26-4b-with-a-causal-encoder-decoder-ced-split-experimental-retrofit \"Spark-X2.6-4B with a Causal Encoder-Decoder (CED) split: experimental retrofit\")\n\n  - [Update 2026-10-29: v2, split moved from layer 18 to layer 24 (recommended)](#update-2026-10-29-v2-split-moved-from-layer-18-to-layer-24-recommended \"Update 2026-10-29: v2, split moved from layer 18 to layer 24 (recommended)\")\n\n- [v1 (2026-10-26): split at layer 18, trained](#v1-2026-10-26-split-at-layer-18-trained \"v1 (2026-10-26): split at layer 18, trained\")\n\n  - [What was trained](#what-was-trained \"What was trained\")\n\n  - [Quality: distillation fidelity](#quality-distillation-fidelity \"Quality: distillation fidelity\")\n\n    - [Next to ordinary quantization](#next-to-ordinary-quantization \"Next to ordinary quantization\")\n\n    - [Task check: GSM8K, 100 questions](#task-check-gsm8k-100-questions \"Task check: GSM8K, 100 questions\")\n\n  - [Speed (same reference implementation for both, bf16, LoRA merged, steady-state median, Intel Arc Pro B70)](#speed-same-reference-implementation-for-both-bf16-lora-merged-steady-state-median-intel-arc-pro-b70 \"Speed (same reference implementation for both, bf16, LoRA merged, steady-state median, Intel Arc Pro B70)\")\n\n    - [Retrieval (\"needle in a haystack\" with 4 near-duplicate distractors, 20 trials per length, greedy)](#retrieval-needle-in-a-haystack-with-4-near-duplicate-distractors-20-trials-per-length-greedy \"Retrieval (&quot;needle in a haystack&quot; with 4 near-duplicate distractors, 20 trials per length, greedy)\")\n\n  - [llama.cpp (GGUF)](#llamacpp-gguf \"llama.cpp (GGUF)\")\n\n  - [Usage](#usage \"Usage\")\n\n  - [Caveats](#caveats \"Caveats\")\n\n  - [Credits](#credits \"Credits\")\n\n# [](#spark-x26-4b-with-a-causal-encoder-decoder-ced-split-experimental-retrofit) Spark-X2.6-4B with a Causal Encoder-Decoder (CED) split: experimental retrofit\n\n## [](#update-2026-10-29-v2-split-moved-from-layer-18-to-layer-24-recommended) Update 2026-10-29: v2, split moved from layer 18 to layer 24 (recommended)\n\nv1 lost a lot of long-context retrieval. The reason turned out to be the split point: the decoder's global layers (23, 27) need information that the layer-18 state simply doesn't contain yet (a closed-form fit from H\\_18 to the original layer-23/27 values leaves 37-48% error, and no projector training fixed it; from H\\_24 it's 6%). v2 moves the split to layer 24: layers 0-23 = encoder (all original weights, including global layer 23), layers 24-35 = decoder, whose 3 global layers (27, 31, 35) read K/V projected from RMSNorm(H\\_24).\n\n**v2 is not trained at all.** The 6 projection matrices are a closed-form ridge-regression fit of the original model's K/V from H\\_24 (about 0\\.5M tokens of PG19 / code / chat / synthetic retrieval); there is no LoRA and every other weight is the original.\n\n|  | v1 (split 18, trained) | **v2 (split 24, no training)** | original |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| long-context generation, 138 items, official scorers: RULER (multi-key needles \\+ variable tracking) | 69% | **88%** | 90% |\n| NoLiMa (36 items) | 8% | **31%** | 33% |\n| BABILong qa1-3 (54 items) | **52%** | 43% | 50% |\n| all 138 | 47% | **55%** | 59% |\n| KL(original ‖ CED), last 512 of 8K, replay 1024 (PG19 / code / chat, 85 held-out seqs) | 0\\.066 / 0\\.095 / 0\\.105 | **0\\.040 / 0\\.083 / 0\\.052** | 0 |\n| top-1 agreement with the original | 0\\.885 / 0\\.922 / 0\\.889 | **0\\.896 / 0\\.937 / 0\\.913** | 1 |\n| needles with 4 near-duplicate distractors, 20 trials at 4K / 8K / 16K | 19 / 18 / 20 | **20 / 20 / 20** | 20 / 20 / 20 |\n| GSM8K, 100 questions, greedy | **87** | 85 | 89 |\n| prefill speedup vs original, PyTorch reference path, replay 1024, 4K / 8K / 16K | **1\\.52 / 1\\.67 / 1\\.72x** | 1\\.35 / 1\\.42 / 1\\.46x | 1x |\n\nThe trade: more of the model runs over the whole prompt (24 of 36 layers instead of 18), so prefill saves about a third instead of about half; in exchange retrieval comes most of the way back to the original. Decode speed is the same as the original either way. Long-context items are thinking-off, greedy, up to 32K tokens; RULER variable tracking is included with the answer prefix pre-filled as in the official setup. llama.cpp prefill on an Intel Arc Pro B70 (Vulkan, Q4\\_K\\_M, llama-server two-phase prefill with replay 1024, ub 512, warm; prompt of repeated token ids, timing only), tokens/s at 4K / 8K / 16K / 32K: original 2867 / 2503 / 1942 / 1332, v1 4234 / 4147 / 3535 / 2595 (1.48 / 1\\.66 / 1\\.82 / 1\\.95x), **v2 3616 / 3358 / 2710 / 1907 (1.26 / 1\\.34 / 1\\.40 / 1\\.43x)**. The PyTorch speedups in the table use the same reference implementation for all variants (the GGUF parity is: mean KL 3\\.5e-4 to 2\\.3e-3 vs the PyTorch path, within about 2x of the plain-GGUF-vs-PyTorch noise floor on the same sequences).\n\n**v2 files:** `spark-ced24-Q8_0.gguf`, `spark-ced24-Q4_K_M.gguf` (GGUF key `spark2_5.ced.split` = 24, same llama.cpp patch as v1, same bartowski imatrix), `ced_weights_split24.safetensors` (the 6 projections, bf16, 31 MB; `demo.py` reads the split from the file). The v1 files below are unchanged.\n\n---\n\n# [](#v1-2026-10-26-split-at-layer-18-trained) v1 (2026-10-26): split at layer 18, trained\n\n> Experimental research artifact, not a drop-in model. Runs in PyTorch (`demo.py`) and in a llama.cpp fork (GGUFs below); stock llama.cpp and vLLM cannot load it.\n\nThis takes the finished [XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](https://huggingface.co/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B) (36 layers, sliding-window layers with a global-attention layer every 4th layer) and converts it to the **CED layout of DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash** ([arXiv 2609\\.19969](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19969), sec. 2\\.2 and 3\\.2.2) *without pre-training anything from scratch*:\n\n- layers 0-17 = **causal encoder** (layers 0-13 are the original weights, 14-17 carry a small LoRA), layers 18-35 = **decoder**;\n\n- the global-attention K/V of the 5 decoder global layers (19, 23, 27, 31, 35) are **projected from the encoder's last hidden state** with layer-dependent weights instead of from each layer's own hidden state: C\\_l = RMSNorm(H\\_18) W\\_l (the paper writes C\\_l = H\\_{L/2} W\\_l; the gain-free RMSNorm on the input is my addition so the projections can be initialised from the layers' own weights);\n\n- decoder sliding-window K/V stay layer-wise and are rebuilt by **bounded replay** of the last prompt tokens.\n\nPrefill runs the encoder (18 layers) over the whole prompt and the decoder (18 layers) over only the last `replay` tokens: close to half the prefill compute. Decode compute and the KV-cache size are unchanged (exact CED keeps one cache per layer; KV compression is CSA2's job, not CED's).\n\n## [](#what-was-trained) What was trained\n\nOnly a small number of weights, by distillation against the original model (KL on its own logits \\+ normalised per-layer hidden-state MSE; no labels, no teacher other than Spark itself), about 50M training tokens in total on one local B70 (\\~6 h):\n\n1. closed-form ridge init of the 10 new K/V projections (5 layers x K,V, 26M parameters) from H\\_18 onto the original layers' K/V;\n\n2. training of those projections;\n\n3.\n\n   - LoRA (rank 16) on all 18 decoder layers;\n\n4.\n\n   - LoRA (rank 16) on encoder layers 14-17 (data: PG19 books, GitHub code, WildChat);\n\n5.\n\n   - 300 more steps with the original model's own sampled answers to GSM8K-train questions and general prompts mixed in (each conversation as its own sequence, full replay).\n\nEncoder layers 0-13 are bit-identical to the original. The LoRA can be folded into the weights (`Spark.merge_lora`), after which decode runs at the original's speed.\n\n## [](#quality-distillation-fidelity) Quality: distillation fidelity\n\n85 held-out 8192-token sequences (44 PG19, 24 code, 17 chat) never used for training or for choosing a checkpoint. KL(original \\|\\| CED) on the last 512 positions, lower is better:\n\n| variant | PG19 (replay 1024) | code | chat | PG19 (replay 512) | code | chat |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| naive init (the layers' own K/V weights on H\\_18) | 0\\.922 | 1\\.613 | 0\\.967 | 0\\.960 | 1\\.716 | 1\\.060 |\n| closed-form ridge init | 0\\.214 | 0\\.492 | 0\\.301 | 0\\.269 | 0\\.561 | 0\\.387 |\n| \\+ trained K/V projections (26M) | 0\\.087 | 0\\.144 | 0\\.134 | 0\\.124 | 0\\.177 | 0\\.187 |\n| \\+ LoRA on the 18 decoder layers | 0\\.076 | 0\\.121 | 0\\.118 | 0\\.105 | 0\\.148 | 0\\.159 |\n| **final: \\+ LoRA on encoder layers 14-17, \\+ on-policy math/chat data** | 0\\.066 | 0\\.095 | 0\\.105 | 0\\.093 | 0\\.122 | 0\\.141 |\n| *floor: original K/V in the decoder, replay only* | 0\\.000 | 0\\.000 | 0\\.000 | 0\\.046 | 0\\.056 | 0\\.077 |\n\n- **Replay of 1024 tokens (2x the 512 window) is lossless** (KL 0\\.000 on top of the K/V approximation); the paper-style replay of exactly one window (512) costs 0\\.05-0.08 KL by itself. Use `replay=1024`.\n\n- Cross-entropy on the last 1024 tokens, original \\-\\> CED (PG19 / code / chat): 2\\.676 \\-\\> 2\\.705 / 0\\.961 \\-\\> 0\\.993 / 2\\.089 \\-\\> 2\\.017. Top-1 agreement with the original: 0\\.885 / 0\\.922 / 0\\.889.\n\n### [](#next-to-ordinary-quantization) Next to ordinary quantization\n\nKL to the unquantized model for the usual GGUF quants ([bartowski](https://huggingface.co/bartowski/Spark-X2.6-4B-GGUF) imatrix quants vs his bf16 GGUF, llama.cpp `--kl-divergence` on CPU, 4K-token chunks, same text domains) next to CED. Not the same positions as the CED numbers (CED: last 512 of 8K sequences), so read it as a rough placement:\n\n|  | PG19 | code | chat | mean |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Q8\\_0 (GGUF, 4\\.38 GB) | 0\\.003 | 0\\.001 | 0\\.001 | 0\\.002 |\n| Q6\\_K (GGUF, 3\\.61 GB) | 0\\.008 | 0\\.002 | 0\\.003 | 0\\.004 |\n| Q5\\_K\\_M (GGUF, 3\\.16 GB) | 0\\.024 | 0\\.010 | 0\\.009 | 0\\.014 |\n| Q4\\_K\\_M (GGUF, 2\\.62 GB) | 0\\.070 | 0\\.027 | 0\\.035 | 0\\.044 |\n| IQ4\\_XS (GGUF, 2\\.34 GB) | 0\\.112 | 0\\.039 | 0\\.049 | 0\\.067 |\n| **CED (this repo)** | **0\\.066** | **0\\.095** | **0\\.105** | **0\\.089** |\n| IQ3\\_M (GGUF, 2\\.23 GB) | 0\\.248 | 0\\.096 | 0\\.104 | 0\\.149 |\n| Q3\\_K\\_M (GGUF, 1\\.99 GB) | 0\\.302 | 0\\.117 | 0\\.156 | 0\\.192 |\n| Q2\\_K (GGUF, 1\\.61 GB) | 0\\.912 | 0\\.420 | 0\\.617 | 0\\.650 |\n| IQ2\\_M (GGUF, 1\\.57 GB) | 0\\.871 | 0\\.482 | 0\\.597 | 0\\.650 |\n\nCED costs about as much as an IQ4\\_XS-to-IQ3\\_M quant. It saves prefill compute, not memory, and the two should stack (errors add up).\n\n### [](#task-check-gsm8k-100-questions) Task check: GSM8K, 100 questions\n\nGreedy, chat template, no thinking, the same PyTorch code path for both: original **89/100**, CED **87/100** (6 questions only the original solved, 4 only CED). A 2-question gap on 100 is within noise.\n\n## [](#speed-same-reference-implementation-for-both-bf16-lora-merged-steady-state-median-intel-arc-pro-b70) Speed (same reference implementation for both, bf16, LoRA merged, steady-state median, Intel Arc Pro B70)\n\nPrefill:\n\n| prompt tokens | original | CED, replay 512 | CED, replay 1024 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 4,096 | 0\\.59 s | 0\\.35 s (1.67x) | 0\\.39 s (1.52x) |\n| 8,192 | 1\\.27 s | 0\\.73 s (1.75x) | 0\\.76 s (1.67x) |\n| 16,384 | 3\\.20 s | 1\\.73 s (1.85x) | 1\\.86 s (1.72x) |\n\nThe reference implementation is plain PyTorch with chunked attention, so absolute tokens/s are not comparable with optimised engines; the ratio is what carries over. The asymptote is \\~2x. Decode at 8K context: original 52\\.4 ms/token, CED 52\\.9 ms/token (LoRA merged). CED is a prefill optimisation, not a decode one.\n\n### [](#retrieval-needle-in-a-haystack-with-4-near-duplicate-distractors-20-trials-per-length-greedy) Retrieval (\"needle in a haystack\" with 4 near-duplicate distractors, 20 trials per length, greedy)\n\n| prompt tokens | original | CED (replay 512) | CED (replay 1024) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 4,096 | 20/20 | 19/20 | 19/20 |\n| 8,192 | 20/20 | 18/20 | 18/20 |\n| 16,384 | 20/20 | 20/20 | 20/20 |\n\n## [](#llamacpp-gguf) llama.cpp (GGUF)\n\n`spark-ced-Q8_0.gguf` (4.4 GB) and `spark-ced-Q4_K_M.gguf` (2.6 GB) have the LoRA merged in and carry the 10 K/V projections as extra tensors (`blk.N.ced_k`, `ced_v`; GGUF key `spark2_5.ced.split`). They need llama.cpp with the CED patch (`llama.cpp-ced.patch` in this repo, against upstream commit `a0e6dc738`; `git apply` it and build as usual, e.g. `-DGGML_CUDA=ON` or `-DGGML_VULKAN=ON`); stock llama.cpp refuses to load them (tensor-count error). The patch adds the CED graph, the converter and a `llama_set_ced` API and does nothing for models without CED tensors. `llama-server -np 1` does the two-phase prefill by itself (replay 1024, `LLAMA_CED_REPLAY=0` turns it off); other slot counts fall back to normal prefill. The quants use bartowski's imatrix, not one of my own.\n\nLogits match the PyTorch CED within the bf16 noise floor (mean KL 3e-4 to 8e-4, CPU and Vulkan); with the decoder sliding-window mask deliberately removed the same test shows KL 0\\.34, so it does catch a broken graph. KL to the *original* bf16 GGUF (mean over PG19 / code / chat, llama.cpp `--kl-divergence`): CED bf16 0\\.067, CED Q8\\_0 0\\.068, CED Q4\\_K\\_M 0\\.114 (plain Q4\\_K\\_M: 0\\.044).\n\nPrefill, tokens/s, Q4\\_K\\_M, warm, ub 512, `-ngl 99`, stock = XHToken's official Q4\\_K\\_M:\n\n| prompt tokens | RTX 4090 stock | RTX 4090 CED |  | Arc Pro B70 (Vulkan) stock | B70 CED |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 4,096 | 18\\.1k | 24\\.3k | 1\\.34x | 3\\.0k | 4\\.4k | 1\\.46x |\n| 8,192 |  |  |  | 2\\.6k | 4\\.2k | 1\\.66x |\n| 16,384 |  |  |  | 2\\.0k | 3\\.5k | 1\\.80x |\n| 32,768 | 14\\.4k | 23\\.4k | 1\\.62x | 1\\.3k | 2\\.6k | 1\\.93x |\n| 131,072 | 8\\.7k | 14\\.7k | 1\\.68x |  |  |  |\n| 262,144 | 5\\.7k | 8\\.3k | 1\\.45x |  |  |  |\n\nDecode (4090, tok/s at 4K / 32K / 128K context): stock 219 / 175 / 93, CED 226 / 177 / 94\\. The 4090 numbers are from a rented host with an i9-14900K; an earlier run on a slow server CPU was CPU-bound (GPU 48-64% busy) and showed almost no gain, so prefill speedups on a weak host will be smaller. The B70 numbers were measured on a build that also has my own Intel Xe2 Vulkan kernel changes (not in the patch here); the stock column is the same build without CED, so the ratio is CED's.\n\n## [](#usage) Usage\n\n```\npython demo.py /path/to/Spark-X2.6-4B ced_weights.safetensors --replay 1024 --compare\n```\n\n`spark_ced.py` is a self-contained PyTorch re-implementation of Spark (checked against the reference code and an fp32 CPU run) with the CED path. `ced_weights.safetensors` holds the 10 K/V projections and the LoRA deltas (bf16, 89 MB); the base weights come from the original repo. In code: `proj, lora = load_ced_weights(...)`, `S.merge_lora(lora)`, then `S.ced_prefill(ids, proj, caches, tail=1024)` and `S.ced_step(...)` with `lora=None`.\n\n## [](#caveats) Caveats\n\n- Not an official or endorsed variant of Spark or DeepSeek; the architecture is from the DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash paper.\n\n- The paper trains CED from scratch with a jointly trained encoder; this retrofit adapts a finished model with LoRA and distillation, so it is lossy (see tables). Greedy outputs diverge from the original after a few dozen tokens; a multi-step arithmetic prompt can come out wrong where the original is right.\n\n- Evaluation is distillation fidelity (KL/CE), 100 GSM8K questions and a synthetic retrieval test, not task benchmarks. The PyTorch speed table above comes from a reference implementation, not an inference engine; the llama.cpp numbers are above.\n\n- GGUF speed numbers use a prompt of repeated token ids (timing only; quality is measured separately); the server path has been checked for identical greedy output on CPU only.\n\n- CED GGUF Q4\\_K\\_M is noticeably lossier than plain Q4\\_K\\_M (KL 0\\.114 vs 0\\.044); Q8\\_0 adds nothing on top of CED.\n\n## [](#credits) Credits\n\nMade by [@imdariotoo](https://x.com/imdariotoo). I post my local-model releases on X, so follow there for the next ones.\n\n**Downloads last month**\n: 194\n\nGGUF[](https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/gguf)\n\nModel size\n\n4B params\n\nArchitecture\n\nspark2\\_5\n\nChat template\n\nHardware compatibility\n\n[Log In](/login) to add your hardware\n\n4-bit\n\nQ4\\_K\\_M\n\n2\\.63 GB Q4\\_K\\_M\n\n2\\.61 GB\n\n8-bit\n\nQ8\\_0\n\n4\\.4 GB Q8\\_0\n\n4\\.39 GB\n\nInference Providers [NEW](https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-providers)\n\n[](/tasks/text-generation \"Learn more about text-generation\")\n\nText Generation\n\nThis model isn't deployed by any Inference Provider. [🙋 Ask for provider support](/spaces/huggingface/InferenceSupport/discussions/new?title=darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED&description=React%20to%20this%20comment%20with%20an%20emoji%20to%20vote%20for%20%5Bdarioooooo0o%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-CED%5D\\(%2Fdarioooooo0o%2FSpark-X2.6-4B-CED\\)%20to%20be%20supported%20by%20Inference%20Providers.%0A%0A\\(optional\\)%20Which%20providers%20are%20you%20interested%20in%3F%20\\(Novita%2C%20Hyperbolic%2C%20Together%E2%80%A6\\)%0A)\n\n## Model tree for darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED [](/docs/hub/model-cards#specifying-a-base-model)\n\nBase model\n\n[XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B-Base](/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B-Base)\n\nFinetuned\n\n[XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B](/XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B)\n\nQuantized\n\n([52](/models?other=base_model:quantized:XHToken/Spark-X2.6-4B))\n\nthis model\n\n## Paper for darioooooo0o/Spark-X2.6-4B-CED\n\n[](/papers/2609.19969)\n\n#### DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash: Pushing the Limits of KV Cache Compression\n\nPaper • 2609\\.19969 • Published Sep 17 • 214\n\nSystem theme\n\nCompany\n\n[TOS](/terms-of-service) [Privacy](/privacy) [About](/huggingface) [Careers](https://apply.workable.com/hugging